  "notes": [
    "Closed clamshell widget/cover displays remain intentionally outside Hero scope.",
    "Responsive smoke cases are geometry checks only; they do not emulate hardware posture APIs.",
    "Chromium is the geometry baseline. Firefox and WebKit are compared against Chromium after absolute containment checks.",
    "The desktop alias selects every desktop-* group, so --group=desktop runs both standard and wide desktop matrices.",
    "Desktop Hero insets are checked for left/right symmetry and a minimum safe edge clearance.",
    "The Hero is intentionally inset; width certification compares .hero with its containing first section while horizontal document overflow is checked independently.",
    "Each browser/viewport run starts in a fresh context at the target geometry so WebKit/WebGL canvas state is not inherited across viewport resizes.",
    "The 7680x2160 case is an extreme CSS-viewport stress probe, not a claim that physical display resolution maps 1:1 to CSS pixels.",
    "1920x1080 is the frozen standard-desktop visual anchor; the wide candidate begins at 1921px so phone/tablet/foldable and <=1920px desktop geometry cannot be affected by the candidate.",
    "Wide-desktop certification now includes minimum visual-scale checks. A run can no longer pass merely because tiny components avoid collisions.",
    "The 2026 Apple geometry group is CSS-geometry validation, not hardware/browser-chrome emulation. iPhone Duo point sizes are inferred from Apple pixel specifications at 3x until measured Safari/TestMU evidence is available."
  ],
  "wideDesktopScale": {
